Title:
adexview — Offline AD Explorer Snapshot Browser, Search, and Audit

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
adexview is a Python tool that converts Sysinternals AD Explorer `.dat` snapshots into a local SQLite-backed dataset and serves an offline web UI for browsing and querying Active Directory data. It solves the problem of analyzing AD structure and attributes without connecting to a domain controller, enabling safe/offline review from any machine (including non-Windows). The viewer decodes and enriches attributes (ACLs/SDDL ACEs, SIDs/GUIDs, timestamps, DNS/DFS data, certificate-related objects) and supports LDAP filter queries (RFC4515 + AD bitwise rules) plus full-text search over decoded values. It includes an “audit” mode that generates findings and reports beyond typical BloodHound coverage (DNS/subnets, DFS targets, GPO links, LAPS/BitLocker exposure, AD CS template issues, trusts, delegation, dangerous ACLs). It’s useful for red teams and pentesters doing AD assessments from collected snapshots, and for blue teams auditing directory hygiene without granting live DC access. Interesting because it operationalizes offline AD analysis with a web UI, report generation, and a CLI query engine while minimizing dependencies (stdlib-only core).

Technical Focus:
- Parsing AD Explorer `.dat` snapshots offline (memory-mapped, bounds-checked)
- LDAP filter parsing/evaluation (RFC 4515, AD bitwise matching rules)
- Attribute decoding/enrichment (security descriptors/ACLs, DNS records, DFS, X.509/AD CS)
- SQLite indexing + search/query planning over snapshot data
- AD security auditing: delegation, ACL abuse, LAPS/BitLocker, trusts, Kerberos settings, AD CS ESC conditions

Use Cases:
- Offline AD reconnaissance and object/attribute review from a captured snapshot
- Rapid LDAP-style querying and exporting results (CSV/JSON/Excel) without DC connectivity
- Generating assessment reports on GPO links, sites/subnets, DNS mappings, DFS shares/targets
- Identifying risky configurations: RBCD/unconstrained delegation, DCSync rights, weak policies, stale accounts
- Auditing AD CS templates for ESC1–ESC5-style misconfigurations and weak keys
- Reviewing LAPS coverage and who can read LAPS passwords; spotting exposed secrets in attributes

Keywords:
Active Directory, AD Explorer, Sysinternals, snapshot parsing, offline analysis, LDAP filters, RFC4515, SQLite, security descriptors, ACL, ACE, DCSync, RBCD, unconstrained delegation, GPO links, DNS records, DFS, LAPS, BitLocker recovery keys, AD CS, certificate templates, ESC1, trusts, SIDHistory, Kerberos encryption types, AS-REP roastable, SPN accounts